from pathlib import Path
import numpy as np
from ..workflow_v04.core import read,polynomial
from ..workflow_v04.report import table,vec
from .core import LABELS

def report(out,summary,ms,cs,os,us,motifs,groups,alignment,worst,refs,barriers):
 lines=['# Why the v0.4 native explanations failed','', 'Post-hoc diagnostic v1, 2026-10-30. Only AND(7,8)→4 and XOR(11,12)→6 were analyzed. All original groups, weights, true biases, contexts and acceptance criteria remain fixed. The saved arrays were reused; CUDA re-inference verified their checkpoint identities. This is diagnosis on already inspected development data, not fresh confirmation.','', '**Observed result:** the failed groups omit signed nonlinear contributions and compensating additive response. AND is dominated by a joint-amplitude error; XOR has substantial baseline, both single-input, and joint errors. Both failures are already present on discovery. The student and native-only targets are sufficiently similar to yield identical small-budget solutions in v0.4, and student guidance did not supply a structural selection constraint. No new selector was run.','', '## What was audited','', 'The exact objective was `mean_(context,corner) (beta[j] + sum_selected R[j,r] h_r − target)^2`, with all four corners equally weighted. Guided target=full student; native-only target=full teacher. Original coefficients were fixed at their saved signed values and original beta was included. Each budget started a separate forward greedy search, followed by at most five improving single swaps. The objective did not require any named component, preserve a positive/negative channel, or separately enforce a joint-interaction loss. We recomputed every recorded intermediate loss, checked final IDs and audited loss changes, without running the selector.','', 'This audit does not find a bias/refitting bug: the loss and exact signed sums reproduce the saved results. A bounded greedy heuristic need not discover complementary units when each alone worsens the residual. The historical-pair algebra below tests one specified example; it is not an optimization over alternative groups. Failure at k≤20 is not proof that no compact explanation exists.','', 'Notation: corner order [00,01,10,11]; polynomial coefficients [baseline, first, second, joint]. `r=teacher−(selected+beta)` is exactly the omitted signed response, so residual means here have the opposite sign to prediction-minus-teacher errors in v0.4. Coefficient sign is distinct from output-write sign: a positive write can have a negative interaction when its response is suppressed by the partner. R=QᵀW2 is an effective semantic output readout, not a hidden-feature decoder.','', '![Existing error curves](fixed_set_errors.png)','']
 for j,name in [(4,'AND(7,8)'),(6,'XOR(11,12)')]:
  lines += [f'## {name}: existing failure explained','']
  table_rows=[]
  for k in [1,3,5,10,20]:
   row=[k]
   for split in ['D','H']:
    for method in ['guided','native_only']:
     r=next(r for r in ms if r['output']==j and r['split']==split and r['method']==method and r['budget']==k);row += [f"{r['corner_mae']:.5f} / {r['joint_mae']:.5f}"]
   table_rows.append(row)
  lines += [table(['k','D guided MAE/joint MAE','D native-only','H guided','H native-only'],table_rows),'','The table uses the original fixed groups on their original discovery (D) and confirmation (H) contexts. Errors on D show failure to approximate even the selection population; the D→H change does not by itself diagnose statistical overfitting.','']
  rows=[]
  for split in ['D','H']:
   for term in ['teacher','selected_with_actual_bias','omitted']:
    vals=[next(r for r in cs if r['output']==j and r['split']==split and r['method']=='guided' and r['budget']==20 and r['term']==term and r['coefficient']==t)['mean'] for t in range(4)];rows.append([split,term,vec(vals)])
   rows.append([split,'mean absolute omitted',vec(summary[str(j)][split]['primary_remainder_abs_alpha'])])
  lines += [table(['split','term','[baseline, first, second, joint]'],rows),'']
  signed=summary[str(j)]['H']['omitted_signed_stats'];lines += ['**Omitted signs and cancellation, guided k20, confirmation:**','',table(['accounting','baseline','first','second','joint'],[[label,*[f'{x:.6f}' for x in signed[key]]] for label,key in [('positive coefficient contributions','positive_mean'),('negative coefficient contributions','negative_mean'),('net omitted','net_mean'),('mean within-context cancellation fraction','cancellation_fraction_mean')]]),'',f"Separate output-write accounting: omitted positive-write corners={vec(signed['positive_write_corner_mean'])}; omitted negative-write corners={vec(signed['negative_write_corner_mean'])}. Their sum is the complete remainder, not an uncertainty band. By write sign, their mean coefficient vectors are {vec(signed['positive_write_alpha_mean'])} and {vec(signed['negative_write_alpha_mean'])}.",'']
  en=summary[str(j)]['H']['primary_metrics'];lines += [f"On the nonorthogonal Boolean basis, the residual's additive-only MSE is {en['additive_MSE']:.6f}, joint-only MSE {en['joint_MSE']:.6f}, and twice their cross term {en['twice_cross']:.6f}; these sum to actual MSE {en['total_MSE']:.6f}. They cannot be interpreted as independent percentages of causal error. In particular a large negative cross term means a partial cancellation, not that the missing terms are unimportant.",'']
  top=summary[str(j)]['H']['top_omitted_units_per_alpha'];rr=[]
  for z,label in enumerate(['baseline','first input','second input','joint']):
   rr.append([label,', '.join(f"{r['unit']}: {r['mean']:+.4f} (|·| {r['mean_abs']:.4f})" for r in top[str(z)][:4])])
  lines += [table(['missing coefficient','largest omitted by mean absolute coefficient: ID: signed mean (mean absolute)'],rr),'','These rankings identify missing accounting terms, not proposed replacement groups. Full positive and negative tables contain every omitted neuron. Small mean terms can still matter through context variation.','']
  for w in [w for w in worst if w['output']==j and w['split']=='H'][:2]:
   lines += [f"**Post-hoc large-error example:** background {w['background_key']} with active coordinates {w['active_background']}, corner {w['corner']}. Teacher={w['teacher']:.6f}, fixed guided k20={w['selected']:.6f}, omitted={w['remainder']:+.6f}. Omitted positive sum={w['positive_omitted']:+.6f}, negative sum={w['negative_omitted']:+.6f}. Remainder coefficients={vec(w['remainder_alpha'])}.",'',table(['omitted neuron','write','q','u,v','gate mask','signed corners'],[[r['unit'],f"{r['write']:.5f}",f"{r['q']:.5f}",f"{r['u']:.5f}, {r['v']:.5f}",r['gate'],vec(r['signed_corners'])] for r in w['top_omitted']]),'']
  align=sorted([r for r in alignment if r['output']==j and r['split']=='H' and r['method']=='guided' and r['budget']==20],key=lambda r:-r['signed_MSE_alignment']);lines += [f"Largest signed error-energy alignments E[v_r·remainder] are "+', '.join(f"{r['omitted_unit']}: {r['signed_MSE_alignment']:+.6f}" for r in align[:4])+'. These sum over all omitted units to the actual MSE. Negative alignments are compensating terms; this accounting does not measure causal necessity or imply that adding a unit would improve a new circuit.','']
  if j==4:
   lines += ['### Why the earlier 224/192/30 trace does not contradict this result','',table(['model/split','unit','in guided k20','write','u,v','q range','11-only frequency','mean joint'],[[r['model']+'/'+r['split'],r['unit'],r['in_guided20'],f"{r['write']:.6f}",f"{r['u']:.6f}, {r['v']:.6f}",vec(r['q_range']),f"{r['11only_fraction']:.4f}",f"{r['mean_alpha'][3]:+.6f}"] for r in refs]),'', 'Native 224 is the positive conjunction-like driver retained by the guided group. Native 192 has an opposing write; native 30 supplies positive response. The two were not inserted into the original group here. Their gates and contributions are diagnostic references from the earlier trace, not prospectively validated global circuit membership. The earlier trace used two training backgrounds and kept a nonzero baseline/additive remainder. v0.4 tested different complete backgrounds and tried to approximate the entire response with one fixed subset and true bias.','',table(['target/stage','MSE','Δloss add192 alone','Δloss add30 alone','Δloss historical pair','2E[v192 v30]'],[[r['method']+'/'+r['stage'],f"{r['MSE']:.6f}",f"{r['192_only_delta']:+.6f}",f"{r['30_only_delta']:+.6f}",f"{r['historical_192plus30_delta']:+.6f}",f"{r['twice_cross_192_30']:+.6f}"] for r in barriers]),'', 'These are loss identities for a pre-named historical pair, not a new group search: ΔL(v)=E[v²−2rv], and ΔL(v192+v30)=ΔL(v192)+ΔL(v30)+2E[v192v30]. A negative pair cross term exposes complementary opposing writes. If both singleton deltas are positive and the pair delta negative, a one-at-a-time forward step cannot take this improving two-unit move. This diagnoses a specific coordination barrier, without proving global optimality, evaluating replacements, or claiming that the historical trio would meet full-response tolerances.','']
  else:
   lines += ['XOR failure includes both missing single-input responses and missing negative joint response. The omitted baseline is negative on average, while the omitted singles are positive: the selected group is too high at 00 and lacks differential rise on the single-input corners. Its joint is insufficiently negative on average, leaving excessive double-input response. The error tables identify the exact omitted unit sources; no lone negative interaction is called a complete XOR mechanism.','']
 lines += ['## Computational motifs in student and original MLP','', 'For each unit, `p=[q,q+v,q+u,q+u+v]`, `h=ReLU(p)`, contribution=`write*h`, and `q=b+sum_nonpair coefficient*background`. All original biases are retained. For u,v>0, a strict 11-only gate requires −(u+v)<q≤−max(u,v). For other signs we use all four inequalities. The full tables retain all 20 input coefficients, nine output writes, q ranges, near-zero margins, every activation, all four signed coefficients and exact gate counts.','', 'We distinguish an instantaneous contextual partition (units can move between gate classes across contexts) from a fixed partition defined by discovery-modal gate and write sign. The fixed partition assigns **every** unit to exactly one class; membership never changes on H. Neither partition optimizes reconstruction or defines a recovered circuit. Mode ties choose the smallest bit mask. Gate masks use weights [1,2,4,8] for [00,01,10,11], so mask8 means string0001.','', '![Mean omitted coefficients](omitted_coefficients.png)','', '![Conditional motif joint accounting](conditional_motif_joint.png)','']
 for j,name in [(4,'AND'),(6,'XOR')]:
  lines += [f'### {name}: signed motif accounting on confirmation','']
  display=[]
  for model in ['native','student']:
   for mask in [8,1,4,2,12,10,15,14]:
    rr=[r for r in motifs if r['output']==j and r['split']=='H' and r['model']==model and r['mask']==mask]
    if not rr:continue
    aa=np.sum([r['mean_alpha'] for r in rr],0);pos=np.sum([r['positive_alpha_mean'] for r in rr],0);neg=np.sum([r['negative_alpha_mean'] for r in rr],0)
    display.append([model,LABELS[mask],f"{sum(r['mean_units_per_context'] for r in rr):.2f}",vec(aa),f'{pos[3]:+.5f} / {neg[3]:+.5f}'])
  lines += [table(['model','conditional gates','mean units/context','[baseline, first, second, joint]','joint positive / negative'],display),'','Not all gate classes are displayed here; the complete partition, including inactive and other signed hinges, is in instantaneous_motifs.csv. Always-on rows have zero joint up to roundoff yet can carry substantial baseline/single terms. A two-corner i-supported gate is not necessarily purely additive: activations at10 and11 can differ, giving a joint coefficient.','']
  fixed=[r for r in groups if r['output']==j and r['split']=='H'];fg=[]
  for model in ['native','student']:
   for r in sorted([r for r in fixed if r['model']==model],key=lambda r:-abs(r['mean_alpha'][3]))[:4]:fg.append([model,LABELS.get(r['discovery_modal_mask'],'mixed'),r['write_sign'],r['unit_count'],f"{r['modal_gate_retention']:.3f}",vec(r['mean_alpha'])])
  lines += ['The largest fixed discovery-modal classes by absolute mean H joint are shown for diagnosis; this post-hoc display rank does not alter any class membership.','',table(['model','D-modal gate','write sign','fixed members','H gate retention','H mean coefficients'],fg),'']
  for model in ['native','student']:
   stats=summary[str(j)]['H'][model+'_signed_stats'];lines += [f"Full {model} positive coefficient sums={vec(stats['positive_mean'])}; negative sums={vec(stats['negative_mean'])}; mean cancellation fractions={vec(stats['cancellation_fraction_mean'])}.",'']
  selected_units=([224,192,30] if j==4 else [r['unit'] for r in sorted([r for r in us if r['output']==j and r['split']=='D' and r['model']=='native'],key=lambda r:-r['mean_abs_alpha3'])[:3]])
  selected_student=([173,266] if j==4 else read(Path('experiments/v2_pairwise_superposition/reports/workflow-v04-20261030/transcoder_hypotheses.json'))['6']['panel'][:3])
  qr=[]
  for model,ids in [('native',selected_units),('student',selected_student)]:
   for u in ids:
    rr=next(r for r in us if r['output']==j and r['split']=='H' and r['model']==model and r['unit']==u);qr.append([model,u,f"{rr['bias']:.6f}",f"{rr['u']:.6f}, {rr['v']:.6f}",f"{rr['write']:.6f}",vec([rr['q_min'],rr['q_max']]),f"{rr['modal_failure_fraction']:.3f}",vec(rr['mean_corners'])])
  lines += [table(['model','unit','true bias','u,v','write','H q range','D-modal gate mismatch','H signed corners'],qr),'']
 lines += ['## Facts, computational hypotheses, and unresolved claims','', '**Observed facts.** AND has opposing positive/negative conjunction-like hinges with different thresholds in both representations. The historical native references and student173/266 support this local family resemblance, with explicit q dependence. The original-v0.4 objective only asked for the student output profile; it never transferred this sign/threshold structure into native selection. XOR is a signed assembly of single-input/missing-partner responses, conjunction-related corrections, 00-only compensation and always-on additive terms. Complete signs and remainders matter in both. Similar net outputs or gate frequencies do not establish identical internal decompositions.','', '**Falsifiable motif hypotheses.** AND: co-active positive and negative 11-only families jointly calibrate joint amplitude, with background-driven offset changes predicting their gate transitions and differential signed writes. XOR: single-input-support families and negative joint/corner-suppression families require companion baseline/single compensation to maintain low00/11 and high01/10. A structure claim predicts which coefficient and corner changes as q crosses a specified gate boundary; it can fail even if total output reconstruction remains good. These are conditional hypotheses, not unique mechanisms or recovered neuron sets.','', '**Could native-only analysis find them?** Yes in principle: C1, b1, R, the same complete-corner contexts and the same gate/sign accounting make these motifs directly enumerable. AND224/192/30 was already identified in the earlier original-neuron trace. There is no matched-budget prospective comparison of motif discovery or held-out gate-transition prediction here. The present output-reconstruction control cannot show that the transcoder contributes information beyond simplifying presentation or directing attention.','', '**Function-level grouping versus largest contributors.** The signed omissions, companion terms and motif partitions support analyzing functional roles jointly instead of treating absolute attribution rank as an explanation. They do not establish one compact fixed functional group, one-to-one correspondence, or causal necessity. Grouping is useful organization of exact accounting; its explanatory or predictive advantage remains an empirical question. Native/student class sizes and amplitudes need not agree, and confirmation-modal failures prevent unconditional detector labels.','', '**What remains unresolved.** We cannot separate optimal compactness limits from this bounded heuristic coordination failures; no optimization was added. Context support is small and conditioned by exclusions (D/H=26/21 for AND,24/23 for XOR), and all contexts are now diagnostically inspected. No new nuisance pairs exist in these two v0.4 H banks. Training overlap persists; stage2 and the earlier two-background trace were exploratory. No claim extends to the protected final population.','', '## One targeted next experiment — prospective XOR motif-transition prediction','', 'Propose a single matched-budget hypothesis-generation test on output6, **not another circuit-selector campaign**. Use the existing frozen models. Before new outputs are inspected, register two arms: student-guided versus native-only. Give both the same discovery input keys, semantic pair, native C1/b1/R access and fixed total component-row inspection budget (for example24 rows, charging student rows against the guided budget). Guided may inspect transcoder components; native-only spends its entire row budget on native components. Keep the same number of hypothesis slots (four) and the same compute/context budget, with no readout fit or group optimization. Model access is the declared treatment; the row/compute budgets must explicitly account for its extra cost. Use independent evaluators or isolated routines so known hypotheses do not leak between arms.','', 'Each arm must lock numerical predictions for named native gate transitions and **all four native motif-contribution coefficients**, including the sign/magnitude of single-input compensation, for matched permitted development backgrounds whose computed q values straddle a declared boundary. Require exact native IDs, boundary, sign and predicted coefficient changes; no generic “XOR-like” label. Freeze both arms before selecting/inspecting outcome tensors. Reserve new permitted input keys through the existing protection and complete-corner split machinery; protect all previously used keys and final/test populations. If there are too few eligible matched contexts, report insufficient support without relaxing exclusions.','', 'Primary comparison: held-out native contribution-profile change MAE across all four corners (equivalently also report each coefficient), plus gate-transition accuracy and failure rate at the fixed inspection budget; score every registered hypothesis, not just successful ones. Always retain full-teacher and omitted-unit accounting. Guidance adds value only if it yields more accurate, specific native predictions at the same budget; similar accuracy favors the simpler native-only route. Because the target is native gate/contribution changes rather than student output reconstruction, this experiment directly addresses the unresolved structural value. This is a proposal only; no new contexts, scoring, selection, or experiment were executed.','', '## Execution and artifacts','', 'Eleven mathematical/CUDA tests passed before diagnostics. CUDA verified both checkpoints, exact P/Q bytes, saved C/R/biases, preactivations, activations, signed contributions and direct outputs on all eight case/model/split combinations, with FP32 reference inference. Every existing selector intermediate objective was recomputed, and source hashes and original locks were verified. Saved-array diagnostics then ran on CPU, as permitted; no silent CPU inference fallback.','', 'Source inputs: [v0.4 summary](../../reports/workflow-v04-20261030/SUMMARY.md), its three case reports, [earlier AND trace](../../traces/and78-two-backgrounds-20261028/report.md), and [Stage2 report](../../reports/stage2-transcoders-20261027/report.md). The latter already reported cancellation and no absolute interaction-sparsity advantage. This analysis explains the subsequent fixed-set failures; it does not overturn those limitations.','', 'Machine-readable tables: fixed_set_errors.csv; coefficient_accounting.csv; omitted_signed_accounting.csv; omitted_error_alignment.csv; objective_trace_audit.csv; AND_historical_references.csv; AND_historical_pair_objective_algebra.csv; unit_summary.csv; instantaneous_motifs.csv; fixed_discovery_motif_groups.csv; and full per-context components_output*.csv. Compressed motif-profile arrays preserve fixed membership and complete responses. worst_errors.json contains the three largest errors for each case/split, including full local thresholds and contributions.','', 'See protocol.json, model_validation.json, objective_audit.json, source_snapshot/, tests.log, execution.log, commands.sh, exit_status, preservation_verified.json and complete.json for provenance and actual process status. The run stops after this report.']
 (out/'REPORT.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
